### What can Future do?

Future (Java 5) provides five operations and nothing else.

```java
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
Future<String> future = pool.submit(() -> settleTrade("TRD-001"));

future.get();                         // blocks until done
future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);     // blocks with timeout
future.isDone();                      // poll: done yet?
future.cancel(true);                  // attempt cancel
future.isCancelled();                 // was it cancelled?

// Cannot do:
// future.thenApply(...)        NO chaining
// future.thenCombine(other)    NO composition
// future.exceptionally(...)    NO error callbacks
// future.complete("manual")    NO manual completion
```

### What does CompletableFuture add?

```java
CompletableFuture<String> cf = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> settleTrade("TRD-001"))
    .thenApply(result -> "Confirmed: " + result)         // chain!
    .thenCombine(fetchTaxAsync(), (r, tax) -> r + tax)   // combine!
    .exceptionally(ex -> "Failed: " + ex.getMessage())   // error!
    .whenComplete((r, ex) -> log(r, ex));                // observe!

// Manual completion:
CompletableFuture<String> manual = new CompletableFuture<>();
manual.complete("Manually set!");
```

### How do they compare?

| Feature | Future (Java 5) | CompletableFuture (Java 8) |
|---|---|---|
| Get result | get() blocks | get()/join() + non-blocking callbacks |
| Chain | No | thenApply, thenCompose |
| Combine | No | thenCombine, allOf, anyOf |
| Error handling | No callbacks | exceptionally, handle, whenComplete |
| Manual completion | No | complete(), completeExceptionally() |
| Async variants | No | thenApplyAsync, supplyAsync |
| Non-blocking | No (block or poll) | Yes (callbacks fire automatically) |
| Creation | pool.submit() | supplyAsync(), runAsync(), new CF() |
| Timeout (Java 9) | No | orTimeout, completeOnTimeout |

### Why is non-blocking important?

With Future, the only way to use the result is to block the calling thread. With CF, you register a callback that fires when the result is ready, and the calling thread is free to do other work.

```java
// Future: caller blocked
Future<String> f = pool.submit(() -> fetchBalance());
// ... caller cannot do anything useful here ...
String balance = f.get(); // BLOCKED until done

// CF: caller free
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchBalance())
    .thenApply(b -> calculateInterest(b))
    .thenAccept(i -> sendStatement(i));
// Caller is free! Pipeline runs in the background.
```

### When should you still use Future?

Future is simpler when you genuinely just need to submit a task and block for the result. For simple fire-and-submit patterns with ExecutorService.submit(), plain Future is sufficient. When you need chaining, composition, or non-blocking behavior, use CompletableFuture.

### What about manual completion?

CompletableFuture can be completed from outside the executing thread. This is useful for testing, integration with callback-based APIs, and bridging different async models.

```java
CompletableFuture<String> cf = new CompletableFuture<>();

// Some callback-based API:
callbackApi.fetchBalance("SAV2001", result -> {
    cf.complete(result); // complete from callback
});

// Or complete with error:
cf.completeExceptionally(new RuntimeException("Timeout"));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does CompletableFuture extend Future? Yes, it implements both Future and CompletionStage.
- Can you use CompletableFuture with ExecutorService.submit()? Not directly; submit() returns Future, not CF. Use CompletableFuture.supplyAsync() instead.
- What is CompletionStage? The interface that defines the chaining and composition methods. CompletableFuture implements it.
